from common import topic

topic('S1',[
('What does exact string matching require at a successful start?','Consecutive text characters equal the entire pattern',['Pattern characters may be reordered','Arbitrary text gaps are allowed','Only the first character must match'],'This searches a substring occurrence, unlike subsequence matching.'),
('For nonempty pattern length m≤n, what zero-based starts are legal in text length n?','0 through n−m inclusive',['0 through n inclusive','1 through n−m only','0 through m−1 always'],'The final pattern character must remain within the text.'),
('If m>n, what should a complete-window matcher return?','No match',['Start zero automatically','Start n','Read beyond the text to check'],'No alignment can hold the whole pattern.'),
('After a naive mismatch, where does the next alignment start comparing the pattern?','Pattern index zero',['At the old mismatch index','At the final character only','At an arbitrary cached suffix'],'The straightforward naive algorithm restarts each candidate window.'),
('For T=ABABABAC and P=ABAC, what is the first zero-based occurrence?','4',['3','2','5'],'T[4..7]=ABAC; all earlier legal starts fail.'),
('For that text/pattern, how many character checks occur at start zero in left-to-right naive matching?','4',['1','3','8'],'A,B,A agree, then pattern C differs from text B.'),
('How many complete windows exist when n=8 and m=4?','5',['4','8','12'],'n−m+1=8−4+1=5.'),
('For T=ABABABAC and P=ABAC, how many character checks occur before the first match is returned?','14',['10','16','20'],'The five alignments cost 4,1,4,1,4 checks.'),
('For text AAAAAAAAAAAA and pattern AAAAC, how many character comparisons occur in a full naive scan?','40',['12','8','60'],'There are 12−5+1=8 windows and every one fails only on its fifth check.'),
('What parameter-sensitive worst-case scan bound applies for 1≤m≤n?','Θ((n−m+1)m)',['Θ(n+m) on every input','Θ(m) independent of windows','Θ(2ⁿ)'],'At most m comparisons per legal window; repeated near-match inputs attain the product order.'),
('If an empty-pattern convention returns zero, what must the implementation avoid?','Reading P[0] before checking m=0',['Returning any integer','Checking text length','Using zero-based indexing'],'An empty pattern has no character at position zero; handle the convention explicitly.'),
('What extra space does a straightforward naive scan need beyond immutable input strings?','O(1) index words',['O(nm) table necessarily','O(2ⁿ) words','O(mn) copies of the text'],'A fixed number of start and comparison indices suffices.'),
('Malfoid finds pattern ACG inside text ATCG by skipping T. Is that an exact substring match?','No; that is a subsequence, not a consecutive occurrence',['Yes because order alone is enough','Yes because both begin A','Only if indices start at one'],'String matching requires adjacent text positions for the full pattern.')])

topic('S2',[
('What does a different Rabin–Karp hash prove when both strings use the same encoding and modulus?','The strings differ',['The strings are equal','There is a collision','The pattern occurs later'],'Equal strings necessarily hash equally; the contrapositive permits rejection.'),
('What must follow equal hashes in the exact version?','Compare actual characters',['Return success immediately','Change the text','Discard the pattern'],'A collision can give equal residues for different strings.'),
('For a length-m positional encoding, what is the leading place value?','d^(m−1)',['dᵐ','mᵈ','d^(m+1)'],'The leftmost symbol is followed by m−1 lower places.'),
('What is Horner evaluation of the next code x?','h←h·d+x',['h←h+d·x always','h←h/d+x','h←x−h'],'Multiplying shifts previous codes one place left, then appends x.'),
('Roll decimal window 36415 to the next window with incoming digit 2. What number results?','64152',['364152','6415','36412'],'Remove 3·10000, multiply the remainder by ten and add two.'),
('What is the general rolling update before reducing modulo q?','(old−out·H)·d+in, H=d^(m−1)',['old−out+in','(old−out·d)·H+in','old·H−out+in'],'Remove the outgoing highest place, shift retained codes and append the incoming code.'),
('With d=10,q=13, what are hashes of decimal strings 25 and 12?','Both 12',['25 and 12','12 and 0','Both 0'],'25 mod13=12 and 12 mod13=12, a deliberate collision.'),
('With d=10,q=13, what is the hash sequence for windows of length two in 1225?','12,9,12',['12,12,12','1,2,2','9,12,9'],'The windows are 12,22,25, whose residues are 12,9,12.'),
('Given old hash 9 for window 22, H=10, outgoing 2 and incoming 5, what is the normalised next hash modulo 13?','12',['−1','9','5'],'((9−20)·10+5)=−105, whose canonical residue modulo13 is 12.'),
('Why precompute H modulo q once?','Its value is the same for every fixed-length window',['The outgoing character never changes','It proves collisions impossible','It removes the need for verification'],'The leading-place factor depends on m,d,q, not the alignment.'),
('How much arithmetic work hashes one length-m pattern by modular Horner evaluation?','Θ(m)',['Θ(m²) necessarily','Θ(1) regardless of m','Θ(2ᵐ)'],'Each symbol performs a fixed number of assumed word operations.'),
('Can increasing a finite modulus eliminate every possible collision among arbitrarily long strings?','No; infinitely many strings map to finitely many residues',['Yes if the modulus is prime','Yes if the base is ten','Only for odd moduli'],'The pigeonhole principle still applies; exactness relies on verifying candidates.'),
('A student returns index zero for P=25,T=1225 solely because both first hashes are 12. What is wrong?','Window 12 collides with 25 and fails character verification',['The modulus must equal the pattern','There are no complete windows','Equal hashes imply equal strings by definition'],'The first digits 1 and 2 differ; the genuine first match is at zero-based index two.')])

topic('S3',[
('What is a spurious hit?','Equal hashes for unequal strings',['A true pattern occurrence','Different hashes for equal strings under the same hash','A window beyond the text'],'The hash filter admits a false candidate that direct comparison rejects.'),
('Does every verified candidate require exactly m character comparisons?','No; a mismatch may occur earlier',['Yes by definition','No candidate compares characters','Only collisions have length m'],'Verification stops at its first mismatch, or after all m characters match.'),
('What work is reused between adjacent Rabin–Karp windows?','The rolling hash',['The entire comparison history without verification','A sorted suffix array','Every possible substring'],'A constant-work update removes the old leading code and adds the next code.'),
('What makes the verified algorithm exact despite collisions?','Every equal-hash candidate is checked against the actual pattern',['Collisions are impossible','Every modulus is infinite','The earliest hash equality is always genuine'],'Hash equality alone is never accepted as proof of string equality.'),
('For P=25,T=1225,d=10,q=13, how many hash-equal candidates are examined before the first match?','2',['1','3','0'],'Windows 12 and 25 hash to12; window22 hashes to9.'),
('For that example, what is the number of character-verification comparisons?','3',['6','2','0'],'The spurious12 costs one mismatch check; the true25 costs two successful checks.'),
('For n=4,m=2, how many rolling updates follow the initial complete-window hash in a full scan?','2',['3','4','1'],'There are three windows, requiring two transitions after initialization.'),
('If h equal-hash candidates are verified, what general word-operation bound includes preprocessing and scan?','O(n+m+hm)',['O(n+m) without assumptions','O(h) independent of pattern length','O(nm²) necessarily'],'Initial hashing and rolling cost linear-scale work; each candidate can add up to m checks.'),
('If h=0 after hashing/preprocessing, what scan-level character-verification work occurs?','Zero',['m at every window','n²','Exactly n+m'],'Different hashes reject all windows without direct comparisons; hashing still costs work.'),
('Why does a single complete window not make preprocessing disappear?','Reading and hashing a length-m pattern/window can still cost Θ(m)',['One window always means m=1','Hash initialization is always free','There are no input characters'],'A small n−m+1 does not erase the initial pattern-length work.'),
('What can create O(nm)-scale worst-case verification work?','Many hash-equal windows that disagree only after long prefixes',['Every hash differing immediately','No legal windows','A zero-length text with positive pattern length'],'Many expensive candidates can erase the filter savings; actual candidate costs matter.'),
('What is needed to claim expected near-linear performance?','Suitable input or hashing assumptions controlling candidate work',['Only calling the prime large','Only measuring one input','Assuming collisions never occur'],'An expectation requires a distribution/model; a fixed finite modulus alone supplies no universal guarantee.'),
('A student counts three hash decisions as three character comparisons in the 1225 example. Why is the reasoning unsound even though both totals happen to be three?','They are different operations whose counts need not agree on other inputs',['Hash comparisons always cost zero','Character comparisons are always hashes','The example has only one window'],'The matching totals are coincidental: verification costs 1,0,2 while each window gets one hash decision.')])

topic('S4',[
('Which direction does the taught Boyer–Moore variant compare within an alignment?','Right to left',['Left to right','Random order','Only the middle character'],'It starts at the pattern endpoint and decrements both comparison pointers on a match.'),
('For one-based pattern P of length m, what charJump is assigned to an absent character?','m',['0','1','−m'],'The table defaults to a full pattern-length mismatch-pointer increment.'),
('What pattern occurrence determines charJump[c]=m−r?','The rightmost occurrence r of c',['The leftmost occurrence always','The number of occurrences','The middle occurrence'],'The rightmost occurrence limits the safe displacement for that character.'),
('After a mismatch jump, what must happen to pattern pointer k?','Reset k=m',['Leave k at the mismatch position','Set k=0 and return success','Decrement k twice'],'A new alignment restarts comparison at the pattern endpoint.'),
('For P=ABCA,m=4, what is charJump[A]?','0',['3','4','1'],'The rightmost A is at r=4, giving4−4=0.'),
('For P=ABCA, what are charJump[B] and charJump[X] for absent X?','2 and 4',['1 and 0','3 and 4','2 and 1'],'B occurs at r=2, so4−2=2; absent X retains4.'),
('At j=2,k=2 with m=4 and mismatching text X absent from P, what simplified jump is max(charJump[X],m−k+1)?','4',['3','7','0'],'max(4,4−2+1)=max(4,3)=4.'),
('In that mismatch state, j advances 2→6. If the old alignment endpoint was4, how far does the pattern slide?','2',['4','6','0'],'The new endpoint6 minus old endpoint4 is2; j had moved left during earlier matches.'),
('Why is a zero charJump entry not permission for a zero alignment shift?','The fallback m−k+1 forces positive endpoint progress',['Zero means the character is absent','The search must stop immediately','Every zero table entry is an error'],'j is at the mismatch, not necessarily the old endpoint; use the full rule.'),
('With m=4,k=2,charJump[T[j]]=0, what simplified mismatch-pointer increment is used?','3',['0','1','2'],'max(0,4−2+1)=3, restoring the old endpoint and moving it one position further.'),
('For one-based successful matching with k=0, why return j+1?','j has moved one position before the matched start',['j remains at the right endpoint','j always equals the pattern length','Every successful start is one'],'Each of m matches decrements both pointers; the final text pointer is immediately before the occurrence.'),
('What preprocessing cost fits an alphabet table of size |Σ| and a length-m pattern?','O(|Σ|+m)',['O(nm) necessarily','O(log m) regardless of alphabet','O(1) for arbitrary alphabet size'],'Initialize each alphabet entry, then scan the pattern to retain rightmost positions.'),
('A student uses j jump4 as the pattern slide after two successful leftward matches. What is wrong?','The matched-suffix length must be subtracted to get alignment slide',['j and the alignment endpoint are always identical','The slide must instead be8','Every jump is measured from the text start'],'Here two matched positions moved j left; pointer increment4 can correspond to alignment slide2.')])

topic('S5',[
('After a mismatch at one-based k in a length-m pattern, which suffix is already matched?','P[k+1..m]',['P[1..k]','P[k..m] including the mismatch','The whole pattern'],'Right-to-left successful comparisons have passed only the positions strictly to the right of k.'),
('What is the length of that matched suffix?','m−k',['k','m−k+1','m+k'],'There are positions k+1 through m inclusive.'),
('What condition qualifies an earlier full suffix occurrence in case1?','Its preceding pattern character differs from P[k]',['Its preceding character must equal P[k]','It must be to the right of the current suffix','Its length must exceed m'],'An equal predecessor would reproduce the already-known mismatch.'),
('If neither a suitable full occurrence nor a prefix overlap exists, what pattern slide is used?','m',['0','k','m−k always'],'Move the pattern beyond the matched suffix; pointer increment still includes prior leftward movement.'),
('For RATSANDCATS,m=11,k=8, what matched suffix is used?','ATS',['CATS','TS','RATS'],'Positions9,10,11 contain A,T,S.'),
('For RATSANDCATS,m=11,k=8, the earlier ATS ends at position4. What is the pattern slide?','7',['4','10','3'],'Align its endpoint4 with old endpoint11: slide11−4=7.'),
('What mismatch-pointer increment corresponds to m=11,k=8 and slide7?','10',['7','3','18'],'Add matched suffix length11−8=3 to the slide7.'),
('If no suitable full suffix occurrence exists, what does case2 seek?','The longest useful suffix that is also a pattern prefix',['Any permutation of matched characters','Only the entire pattern reversed','The smallest alphabet character'],'Aligning this prefix preserves the largest possible remaining overlap.'),
('For WOWWOW in increasing k=1..6, what is the course matchJump table?','[8,7,6,7,3,1]',['[3,3,3,5,2,1]','[1,3,7,6,7,8]','[6,6,6,6,6,6]'],'The first distractor is the slide table; add m−k to each slide to obtain pointer increments.'),
('For WOWWOW at k=4, why is the earlier full OW occurrence excluded?','It has the same preceding W as the mismatch position',['OW is not a suffix','The pattern has no W','Every full occurrence is forbidden'],'That alignment repeats the known mismatching predecessor. Prefix overlap W instead supplies slide5.'),
('For m=6,k=4 with slide5, what is matchJump[k]?','7',['5','2','11'],'The pointer moved left across a suffix of length2, so2+5=7.'),
('Why can a good-suffix table from another implementation be unsafe to copy directly?','Its indices or values may describe alignment slides rather than this pointer increment',['All implementations have identical table semantics','Table order never matters','Good-suffix shifts are random'],'Both index direction and measured displacement must match the scan code.'),
('A student reads the slide table [3,3,3,5,2,1] directly as matchJump for WOWWOW. What is missing?','Add m−k to each entry',['Multiply every entry by m','Reverse the text instead','Subtract every entry from zero'],'This code jumps from the actual mismatch pointer, which is already left of the old endpoint.')])

